  <h2>Common sewer line materials</h2>
  <p>
    Older Puget Sound homes commonly have clay, cast iron, or Orangeburg (bituminous fiber) sewer laterals - all of which degrade differently with age. Newer installations typically use PVC, which holds up considerably longer.
  </p>

  <h2>What causes most sewer failures</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Root intrusion at pipe joints, especially with clay and cast iron</li>
    <li>Pipe bellies (low spots) that collect waste and cause backups</li>
    <li>Full material breakdown, common with Orangeburg pipe from the mid-20th century</li>
    <li>Ground shifting or nearby construction disturbing the line</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How we diagnose before recommending repair</h2>
  <p>
    We always start with a <a href="/drain-camera-scoping/">camera inspection</a> to see the pipe's actual condition before recommending a fix - whether that's cabling, <a href="/hydrojetting/">hydro jetting</a>, or full <a href="/sewer-line-repair-replacement/">repair or replacement</a>, including <a href="/trenchless-sewer-line-repair-replacement/">trenchless options</a> that avoid excavating your whole yard.
  </p>

  <h2>Learn more</h2>
  <p>
    Browse our <a href="/video-blog/">blog</a> for deeper dives on specific sewer topics, including how to tell if a line has collapsed and whether trenchless repair is right for your situation.
  </p>
